# Querying & Mutating the Board

Three primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern, plus CLI for humans:

## Concrete Assignment Default

When a prompt already supplies a Task/Bug ID and task inputs, query only fields
needed for the next decision:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status checklist outcomeResources }'
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'
```

Do not add routine `summary()`, `plan()`, `schema()`, or `{ full }` orientation.
Use `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)` only after an unknown
call. Board-wide observability and plans belong to an orchestrator routing
decision; full reads remain explicit audit/debug surfaces.

## DSL Queries — primary read approach for agents

Compact queries that return structured output. Zero session overhead (no MCP handshake), batch multiple queries in a single call. **This is what agents should use for all structured reads.**

**Output format:** Use `--format compact` for token-efficient output (~40% fewer tokens). Default is `--format json`.

```bash
# Board overview
task-board q --format compact 'summary()'

# Whole-project readiness with the existing summary folded in
task-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'

# Scoped readiness at each board level
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'
task-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260211-def) { observability-story }'
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { observability-task }'

# Single element lookup
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260211-abc) { status assignee }'

# Bounded activity history (continue with cursor=<next_cursor>)
task-board q --format compact \
  'activity(TASK-260211-abc, limit=25, order=descending, kinds="progress,content", view=effective)'

# Filtered listing with field preset
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'

# Agent monitoring
task-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'

# Batch: multiple queries in one call (semicolon-separated)
task-board q --format compact 'get(T1) { status }; get(T2) { status }; get(T3) { status }'

# Zero-argument and parameterized operations can share a batch
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(); models(agent=codex)'

# Execution plan
task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'

# Schema introspection — discover available fields, presets, operations, and mutations
task-board q 'schema()'
task-board q 'schema(operation=project_config)'
task-board q 'schema(mutation=set_status)'

# Target-only spawn policy (explicit opt-in in every profile)
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

**Operations:** `get(ID)`, `activity(ID, cursor?, limit?, order?, kinds?, view?)`, `list(filters...)`, `count(filters...)`, `summary()`, `plan(scope?, mode=?, active=?)`, `agents(stale=N?)`, `models(agent?)`, `project_config(view?, role?, agent?)`, `whoami()`, `schema(operation?|mutation?)`
**Field projection:** `{ id status assignee }`; base presets: `minimal`, `default`, `overview`, `full`, `progress`, `planning`; observability presets: `observability-project`, `observability-epic`, `observability-story`, `observability-task`
**Batching:** semicolons between queries. Statements and results preserve source order. JSON batches return an ordered top-level array; compact batches format each result normally and separate adjacent results with one blank line. A single query remains unwrapped. Syntax and unknown-operation errors reject the whole batch before execution instead of returning partial output; handler errors remain ordered in-band error results and later statements continue. Element mutations in one batch run inside one activity transaction: when any statement is refused, the refused statements keep their own errors, every statement that would otherwise have succeeded is reported as `ok:false` with code `batch_rolled_back`, and nothing is persisted. A clean batch commits every statement and records one activity event per affected element, so a batch may change an element's checklist only once; issue further checklist changes as separate commands. Local and remote mutation commands both print that complete envelope before returning a nonzero exit, so the envelope always describes exactly what was persisted.
**Format:** `--format compact` (agent default) or `--format json` (TUI/programmatic)

**First-time setup:** Under the full profile, `schema()` returns the complete API contract. Under lite, skip the proactive full read and request only the failed/unknown call with `schema(operation=NAME)` or `schema(mutation=NAME)`. Parser errors for a known call include that same typed signature directly.

`activity()` is deliberately separate from element projections: ordinary
`get`/`list` reads never embed history. Its `limit` defaults to 50 and is bounded
to 1..100. `order` accepts `ascending` or `descending`, `view` accepts `raw` or
`effective`, and `kinds` accepts a comma-separated operation filter. Reuse only
the opaque `next_cursor` returned by the previous page and keep the same order;
the cursor is direction-bound and sequence-anchored for stable traversal across
concurrent appends. The local CLI and remote `/q` endpoint share this contract.
Activity is automatic infrastructure output; callers must not append routine
history themselves. An explicit `legacy_pre_feature` boundary means older
events are unavailable, not that they should be inferred. Deletion retains the
terminal typed history. Operators correct a bad record only with confirmed,
append-only `correct_activity`; the original remains in `view=raw`, while
`view=effective` applies the correction, and remote correction requires Admin.
Never replace bounded pages with `get(...){full}` or an unbounded filesystem
read.

`kinds` also selects the three DERIVED control-plane planes. `run` returns the
spawn run lifecycle mirrored onto the run's task, `change_request` returns the
Change Request transitions mirrored onto the element the CR is attached to, and
`workspace` returns the Story workspace operations mirrored onto the Story:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'activity(TASK-260211-abc, kinds="run,change_request", limit=25)'
task-board q --format compact 'activity(STORY-260211-abc, kinds=workspace, limit=25)'
```

These events carry one typed `ledger_event` change — domain, ledger id,
enumerated event token, sequence, revision, from/to state tokens, a UTC
instant, and typed `oid`/`ref`/`id`/`token` facts. They never carry a summary,
message or reason: each ledger keeps its own human-readable text and the mirror
cannot transport it. The default unfiltered view includes them alongside board
mutations, and filtering by a board kind such as `kinds=progress` returns
exactly what it returned before the planes existed.

The runtime emits them automatically at the moment each ledger row is written.
`record_ledger_event` exists so the local and remote transports share one
statement and one validator, and it refuses any free-text payload.

**It is not an agent-facing mutation.** `task-board m 'record_ledger_event(...)'`
is refused, on a local and a remote board alike, with a
`ledger_mirror_unauthorized` error: a mirror is DERIVED from a ledger row, so
one appended by hand asserts a control-plane fact that never happened. On a
local board the refusal is enforced twice over — once on the CLI mutation
surface and once in the shared activity layer, which admits only the runtime's
derived-mirror sink as the source entrypoint.

**Stated bound — board-server boards.** The prevention above is a property of
the CLI, not of the server. A mirror bound for a remote board is relayed over
the same `m` transport, authenticating as the same principal, through the same
handler entrypoint, as any other mutation that principal could send, so the
server cannot tell a relayed mirror from one a write-capable principal composed
by hand — and it admits both. What the remote plane holds instead is
attribution: every mirror records the authenticated principal that sent it, so
a hand-minted one is traceable to its author even though it is not prevented.
Preventing it would need per-record attestation the transport has no field for;
admin-gating the operation would break the ordinary agent's mirror, which is
the behaviour the remote plane exists to provide.

**Server capability is proven, never assumed.** Before sending a mirror to a
board-server the CLI reads `/v1/version` and requires the `ledger_mirror`
contract. A server that advertises none, or a different version, is refused
with a message naming what it advertised — a server that cannot persist a
mirror would refuse `record_ledger_event` as an unknown operation, which looks
exactly like a mirror that was never attempted. A `/version` read that FAILS is
refused too, and separately: a failed read is not an absence, and treating it
as one would silently drop mirrors a capable server would have accepted.

Run heartbeats are deliberately not mirrored, and run `progress` rows are
mirrored only when the project sets `validation.mirror_run_progress_rows=true`
— see `docs/activity-mutation-coverage.md` for those bounds and for what
`task-board validate` reports when a ledger row has no mirror.

### Scoped signatures

`schema(operation=NAME)` and `schema(mutation=NAME)` return one flat signature object: `kind`, `name`, a typed `signature`, description, parameters, and examples; mutations also report `destructive` and `idempotent`. They do not include unrelated fields, presets, operations, or mutation contracts. Provide exactly one scope. `schema()` without a scope retains the full legacy response.

Known-operation parser failures preserve the underlying typed parse error and append the relevant signature. This lets an agent repair a call without reading the full schema.

### Recovery examples from the Story binary

Captured at Story commit `13afc672f01a38d5748cca533f33524aec8080d9`.
From the repository root, build without installing:

```bash
(cd tools/board-cli && go build -o ../../.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board .)
```

The commands below use that binary and the authoritative board selected by
`TASK_BOARD_DIR`. IDs refer to this Story's board; substitute existing IDs on
another board. Output blocks quote the exact emitted text (errors are stderr).
Exit 1 is an expected refusal, not a passing command.

#### Unknown operation

An unknown operation lists the known query operations and points to `schema()` for discovery and `schema(operation=NAME)` for one signature.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'fetch()'
```

Output (exit 1):

```text
query error: parse error at 1:1: unknown operation "fetch" (got "fetch"); known operations: activity, agents, context, context_boundary, context_candidates, context_retention, contexts, count, get, list, models, plan, project_config, provider_limits, schema, summary, whoami; see schema() for the contract and schema(operation=NAME) for one signature
```

#### Statement grammar and bare positional arguments

The scoped signature includes `syntax`: the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument, and subsequent arguments use `key=value`. Thus `get(TASK-260907-bg79el)` matches `get(id:string)`.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'schema(operation=get)'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"description":"Get a single element by ID","examples":["get(TASK-XX) { overview }"],"kind":"operation","name":"get","parameters":[{"name":"id","type":"string","optional":false,"description":"Element ID"}],"signature":"get(id:string)","syntax":"OPERATION(element-or-identifier, key=value, key=\"quoted string\") { field preset }; statements separated by ';'; the first bare positional value is the element/identifier argument; remaining arguments use key=value (no operators or lists); string escapes: \\\" \\\\ \\n \\t"}
```

#### Unknown projection field

A misspelled projection field suggests `updated` and points to `schema()` for valid fields and presets.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'get(TASK-260907-bg79el) { updated_at }'
```

Output (exit 1):

```text
query error: parse error at 1:29: unknown field "updated_at" (got "updated_at"); did you mean `updated`?; see schema() for valid fields and preset names
```

#### Bare element ID

An element ID alone is refused; wrap it in `get(ID) { fields }`.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board q 'TASK-260907-bg79el'
```

Output (exit 1):

```text
query error: an element ID is not an operation; use get(ID) { fields }
```

#### Keyword IDs on mutations

Every id-first mutation accepts `id=...` (bare or quoted) as well as the first positional argument. This includes `link`, `add_checklist_item`, and `set_status`; supplying both forms is rejected. These successful dry runs preview without persisting changes.

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'link(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, blocked_by=TASK-260211-1saw6h)'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"ok":true,"result":{"blocked_by":"TASK-260211-1saw6h","dry_run":true,"id":"TASK-260907-bg79el"}}
```

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'add_checklist_item(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, text="Verify recovery guidance")'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"ok":true,"result":{"dry_run":true,"id":"TASK-260907-bg79el","text":"Verify recovery guidance"}}
```

```bash
.temp/TASK-260907-bg79el/task-board m --dry-run 'set_status(id=TASK-260907-bg79el, status=development)'
```

Output (exit 0):

```text
{"ok":true,"result":{"dry_run":true,"id":"TASK-260907-bg79el","status":"development"}}
```

### `project_config()` runtime shape

`project_config()` is always resolved by the local CLI, including when normal
board reads and writes target a remote server. Its current top-level keys are
`configured_mode`, `effective_mode`, `board_source`,
`explicit_remote_override`, `project_root`, `config_path`, `config_exists`,
`local`, `remote`, `whoami`, `goals`, `spawn`, `codex`, and `version_control`.

The no-argument shape remains the role-optional general config surface in every
profile, including the full default. For a spawn decision, opt in explicitly:

```bash
task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
```

`role` is required. `agent` is optional and otherwise follows the same
preferred-provider/schedule/runtime-affinity resolver as `task-board spawn`.
The narrow result contains `enabled`, `max_parallel`, a small effective
agent-context profile/source header, the effective allowed and target providers,
the active schedule window, canonical role, merged role ceiling for the target
provider, provider selection mode, and launch-composition enabled/status. It
excludes local/remote auth, `whoami`, Codex session, nested agent-context policy,
project paths, all unrelated provider ceilings, and version-control fields.
Active schedule allow-sets and provider refusals use the same fail-closed
resolver as spawn.

For Codex, `resolved_role_ceiling` includes the resolved boolean `fast_mode`,
`fast_mode_source` (`default` or `configured`), and `provenance.fast_mode` with
the exact root or remote key. Durable spawn status and JSON run state carry
`service_tier`, `fast_mode`, `fast_mode_source`, and `fast_mode_config_key`;
provider-limit retries update them to describe the provider currently being
launched, while each `provider_attempts[]` entry preserves the tier evidence
carried by that historical launch.

The lite profile's resolved `queries.project_config=spawn-preflight` setting
guides agents toward the target-specific call; it does not rewrite a bare
`project_config()` invocation. Supplying a view or target argument opts into
the narrow projection, whose role remains mandatory.

The policy members relevant to spawning and status mutation are:

| Path | Shape |
|---|---|
| `spawn.preferred_agent` | Legacy configured provider string, or `null`. It is retained only as compatibility evidence. |
| `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` | Currently effective `{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}`, or `null`. During an active schedule window, `source` is `schedule`. |
| `spawn.schedule` | `{configured, timezone, config_key, windows, active_window, base_preferred_agentic_system}`. `timezone` is always `UTC`; every projected window also carries its normalized bounds, key, and provider-system projection. |
| `spawn.launch_composition` | `{enabled, config_key, contract, schema_version, degradation}` without invoking `agents-infra`. |
| `spawn.ceilings` | Always reports effective `contract_version`, `authority`, `configured`, and `config_path`. V3 providers expose normalized `allowed_models` or `entries`, nullable `resolution_model`, confirmation mode, effort direction, merged role-effective policy, and exact provenance. Every contract version and input form reports `admitted_pairs`, the canonical admitted `(model, effort)` set with its digest — the field to read when deciding what a policy admits, since `allowed_models` is `null` under the entries form. An entries policy also reports `entries_expanded`, `entries_expansion_pinned`, and any unreachable/redundant entry `warnings`. V2 exposes `snapshot_version`, `snapshot_allowed_models`, `migration_required`, stable actionable warnings, and exact read-only v3 `migration` objects. |
| `spawn.selection` | Required flags plus provider effort vocabularies and supported model criteria. |
| `spawn.execution` / `spawn.tracking` | Executable background mode and durable run-observation contract. |
| `goals` | Static compact `board-goal-v2` capability with required Session Manager native reflection and the one-release `primary-goal-v1` / `board-goal-v1` adapters. The legacy `goals.primary` descriptor remains nested alongside the v2 fields for the compatibility release. It never embeds the current objective and is omitted from `view=spawn-preflight`. |
| `version_control` | `{configured, confirm, desired_commit_time, enforced_types, owner_message, acknowledgement_parameter, acknowledgement_value, config_key}`. |

Local mode uses `spawn.*` keys. Remote mode uses `remote.spawn.*` when that
object is present and non-null; otherwise it falls back to the whole root
`spawn` object. The reported `spawn.config_key_prefix` and nested `config_key`
values always identify the effective source. `preferred_agentic_system` is the
canonical config spelling; `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.

`spawn-policy-v3` admission is exact membership in the effective
`allowed_models` set. `PolicyRank`, lifecycle, recommendation, registry order,
and price are not policy inputs. A v2 projection is compatibility evidence,
not an automatic migration: setup, query, spawn, and install never rewrite the
repository. For an explicit migration, copy the full projection's provider and
role `migration` objects under `contract_version: spawn-policy-v3`, review the
diff, then repeat full and target-role preflight queries. An effort-only v2
warning uses reason `spawn_policy_v2_effort_only_migration_required` and
explicitly says to omit `resolution_model` unless a fallback is intended.

### Project observability preset family

`summary() { observability-project }` returns the whole-project view. The
element presets work with the existing `get` and `list` operations; the task
preset also applies to Bugs because Tasks and Bugs share the leaf estimate
contract.

Every preset exposes the same nested `observability` field:

- `scope` identifies the project or selected Epic/Story/Task/Bug.
- `summary` carries the existing `byType`, `active`, and `blocked` summary,
  scoped to that element and its descendants.
- `readiness.estimateKind` carries the leaf or calculated
  Story/Epic/project projection state without dumping every missing leaf.
- `readiness.coverage` always reports `estimated`, `unestimated`, `total`,
  `ratio`, and `complete`, including fully estimated scopes.
- `readiness.rolledEstimate` is the known Fibonacci sum even when coverage is
  partial. Query the separate `estimate` field on the incomplete Story/Epic to
  retrieve its exact `unestimatedTaskIds` drill-down list.
- `isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard dependency state across the
  scope, while `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` aggregate
  derived cross-container waiting. The nested summary retains blocker details.
- Only the project preset adds `primary_goal`. Its active form is
  `{active:true,id,revision,objective,updated_at}`; after a clear or before
  any creation it is `{active:false,latest_ref}` (where `latest_ref` can be
  `null`). Epic, Story, Task, and Bug observability deliberately do not repeat
  this board-wide parent intent or treat it as element acceptance.

Plain `summary()` keeps its legacy response shape for compatibility.

## DSL Mutations — structured writes for agents

Write operations through the same DSL engine. All mutations support `--dry-run` preview. Destructive mutations require `--confirm`.

Element-ID mutations accept the first positional argument or `id=ID` (bare or quoted), matching their schema signatures: `link(ID, blocked_by=OTHER)` and `link(id="ID", blocked_by=OTHER)` are equivalent. Supplying both forms is rejected. `create(type=..., parent=...)` retains its keyword syntax.

```bash
# Status changes
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=development)'

# Evidence-backed role handoff (preferred producer end transition)
task-board handoff TASK-12 --role developer

# Task/Bug estimate enum
task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=notEstimated)'
task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-12, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'

# Create elements
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task)'
task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-05, name=new-task, description="Implement feature", scope="Backend only", ac="Tests pass")'

# Update README fields
task-board m 'set_details(TASK-12, title="New title", description="Updated desc")'

# Rename overview name/slug while preserving ID, README, progress, children, and resources
task-board m 'rename(TASK-12, name=better-task-name)'

# Assignment
task-board m 'assign(TASK-12, agent=claude-opus)'
task-board m 'unassign(TASK-12)'

# Dependencies
task-board m 'link(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'
task-board m 'unlink(TASK-13, blocked_by=TASK-12)'

# Checklist
task-board m 'check_item(TASK-12, item=1)'
task-board m 'uncheck_item(TASK-12, item=1)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-12, text="Write tests")'

# Notes
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text="Progress update")'        # append
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-12, text="Replace all", set=true)'  # replace

# Move to new parent
task-board m 'set_parent(TASK-12, parent=STORY-03)'

# Resources
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md", type=precondition, content="...", description="Design doc")'
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md", type=precondition, content="revised", description="Design doc v2")'
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-12, name="TASK-12_design.md")'

# Destructive — preview first, then confirm
task-board m --dry-run 'remove_checklist_item(TASK-12, item=2)'
task-board m --confirm 'remove_checklist_item(TASK-12, item=2)'
task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-12)'
task-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-12)'

# Batch mutations (semicolon-separated)
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-12, status=done); set_status(TASK-13, status=development)'
```

**Safety flags:**
- `--dry-run` — preview mutation without applying. Returns what would change
- `--confirm` — required for destructive mutations (`delete`, `delete_resource`)
- Mutation arguments are schema-checked. Unknown named arguments and extra positional fragments are rejected with typed errors before any write runs.
- Without `--confirm`, destructive mutations are rejected with an error

**Format:** `--format json` (default) or `--format compact`

**Introspection:** Run `task-board q 'schema(mutation=NAME)'` for one mutation signature, or full `schema()` for the entire contract.

`task-board handoff` resolves the role's configured end status, verifies all
checklist items plus an actual stored task-scoped outcome, and serializes that
decision with the ordinary status transition. The underlying shared `handoff`
mutation carries the already-resolved role/status for remote execution; use the
CLI facade so callers do not have to reproduce role-policy resolution.

The `estimate` q field is a nested enum. Task/Bug values are either
`{"kind":"notEstimated"}` or
`{"kind":"estimated","scale":{"kind":"fibonacci","number":5}}`.
Story/Epic values are calculated, never persisted or hand-set. Full coverage
returns the same `estimated` shape with the descendant sum. Partial coverage
returns
`{"kind":"partiallyEstimated","rolledEstimate":{"kind":"estimated","scale":{"kind":"fibonacci","number":5}},"notEstimatedCount":"1/3","unestimatedTaskIds":["TASK-..."]}`.
Story counts direct Task/Bug children; Epic rolls up descendant Task/Bug values.
New Task/Bug records must be estimated before entering `development`; legacy
records with no persisted Estimate section remain compatible and count as
unestimated in container projections.

## Grep — unstructured text search

When DSL can't help — searching for arbitrary text across board files.

```bash
# Find mentions of a term
task-board grep "AudioRecorder"

# Search in specific file type
task-board grep "blocked.*deployment" --file progress.md

# Case-insensitive search in descriptions
task-board grep "TODO" -i --file README.md

# With context lines
task-board grep "error" -C 2
```

## CLI — human-facing output and complex operations

CLI text output with ANSI colors is designed for humans. Use for operations not covered by DSL, or when human-readable output is needed.

When reporting board state to a person, reference each epic, story, task, or bug with both its concrete ID and its human-readable title/name. In practice, that usually means carrying both `id` and `name` in your query output, or using a preset such as `default` or `overview` that already includes them. ID-only references are not enough for chat summaries because readers should not have to resolve identifiers manually.

```bash
# Good human-facing summary inputs
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { id name status }'
task-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'
```

### Validate: uncommitted board state

`task-board validate` reports `board_state_uncommitted` for every `done`
element whose lane-class board paths differ from HEAD in the control-root
working tree. Each row carries the element, `due`, the exact dirty paths, and
— on due rows only — the `task-board board commit <STORY-ID>` remedy. `due`
is true only when no lifecycle command will record the bytes: a done Story or
Epic, or a done leaf under a done or closed parent. A done leaf under an open
parent is informational (`due:false`, no remedy): its bytes ride the parent's
close. Severity is warning, exit code 0; the diagnostic never blocks. After a
successful local `handoff` that leaves a due row for any done element, the
handoff result carries the same rows as `uncommitted_advisory` and still
succeeds.

Stated bounds: remote mode omits the diagnostic (no control-root working tree
there); so do non-git checkouts and boards outside the control root. The
diagnostic compares working tree against HEAD only — committed-but-unpushed
board state is publication debt, owned separately, and a clean diagnostic
says nothing about it. Dirt owned by a non-done element is reported when
that element reaches done, not before.

### Parent alignment in the unified goal ledger

Use the top-level `goal` group to maintain one provider-neutral, board-scoped
statement of the current `parent/primary` owner's complete objective. It
survives a parent-session replacement, records immutable `board-goal-v2`
revisions, and never changes a board element's status or acceptance evidence.
The command group is the one-release `primary-goal-v1` compatibility adapter.

```bash
# Create only when no primary goal is active.
task-board goal set-primary \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements." \
  --reason "First actionable requirement"

# CAS-replace the whole objective. Repeating the same objective is idempotent
# once the installed row already carries the routing-duty condition; on a board
# upgraded with a pre-fix goal it mints the forward-looking default instead.
task-board goal update --if-revision 1 \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements and active delivery work." \
  --reason "Requirement expanded"

# Optional: override the native evaluator condition the bound parent session
# is installed with. The default is the forward-looking routing-duty
# condition (every terminal run notice routed, no dispatchable leaf
# undispatched, or explicitly waiting on a human) — a condition that is
# still unmet at install time, unlike the retired "has handled this
# revision" acknowledgement. Non-empty, no colon; omitted on update keeps
# the active revision's condition.
task-board goal update --if-revision 2 \
  --objective "Keep the parent goal aligned with the current user requirements and active delivery work." \
  --provider-condition "Every review chain on this board has a filed verdict and no leaf is unassigned" \
  --reason "Board-specific remaining duty"

# Read current state, immutable audit history, or one immutable revision.
task-board goal get
task-board goal get --history
task-board goal get --goal-id PRIMARY-GOAL-260723-a1b2c3 --revision 1

# Clear only with the observed revision and an explicit disposition reason.
task-board goal clear --if-revision 2 --reason "Parent intent superseded"
```

`goal get` is read-only and remains available inside a spawned run. When
`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, `set-primary`, `update`, and `clear` return the
typed `primary_goal_parent_context_required` failure with no side effects;
spawned owners must use `task-board spawn goal` or `task-board spawn goal
upsert` for their `run/RUN-*` owner. Both commands use `board-goal-v2`; owner
isolation prevents either compatibility adapter from silently updating or
clearing the other.

Every successful JSON response has `ok`, `board_scope`, `primary_goal`, and
`latest_ref`; mutations additionally carry `mutation`. Exact revision reads
add `primary_goal_revision`, and history reads add newest-first `history`
summaries. Typed failures use
`{ok:false,error:{code,message,current_ref,side_effects:false,remediation?}}`.
Remote primary-adapter mutations accept authoritative `goal=board-v2` or the
one-release `primary_goal=board-v1` compatibility capability. Direct v2
mutations require `goal=board-v2`, and reads never create a local shadow
ledger.

## Decision table

| Need | Tool | Example |
|------|------|---------|
| Discover full API contract | DSL | `task-board q 'schema()'` |
| Repair one operation/mutation call | DSL | `task-board q 'schema(operation=NAME)'` / `schema(mutation=NAME)` |
| Preflight one spawn target | DSL | `task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER)'` |
| Structured query (status, filter, lookup) | DSL query | `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'` |
| Structured write (status, assign, link, create) | DSL mutation | `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-XX, status=development)'` |
| Evidence-backed producer handoff | CLI facade over shared mutation | `task-board handoff TASK-XX --role developer` |
| Board parent intent | CLI primary-goal group | `task-board goal get` / `task-board goal update --if-revision N ...` |
| Preview write without applying | DSL mutation | `task-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-XX)'` |
| Text search across elements | Grep | `task-board grep "AudioRecorder"` |
| **Phase planning (canonical API)** | **DSL query** | **`task-board q 'plan(EPIC-XX, mode=children)'`** |
| **Pretty-print / render / save a plan** | **CLI facade** | **`task-board plan EPIC-XX --render --format png`** |
| Resource management | DSL mutation / CLI | `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-XX, name=TASK-XX_results.md, content="...", type=outcome)'` |

### Plan output fields

Execution order comes from the task/bug graph: `plan.phases[].elements` lists
tasks and bugs only, `plan.summary.totalElements` counts them, and
`plan.criticalPath` carries their IDs. Each phase also carries `containers`,
the ancestor story/epic IDs for that wave in parent-walk order; a story with
tasks in several waves appears in each wave's list. `plan.task_cycles` is
always present (an empty array when none exist); each entry contains task/bug
`members`, stored `links`, a closed dependency `chain`, and an `outside_scope`
flag. A cycle inside the requested scope refuses the plan with a
`task_dependency_cycle` error envelope carrying the chain instead of an order;
a cycle outside the scope is listed with `outside_scope: true` and the plan
still succeeds. Compact output (`q --format compact`) emits the same task-ID
`waves` in phase order, the critical path as one `A > B` line, the same
`task_cycles` array, and one advisory line when projection cycles exist.
`task-board plan` (human print, `--save`, `--render --layout phases|hierarchy`)
presents the same waves, per-wave containers and diagnostics as the canonical
JSON. `plan --json` on a cycle refuses on stderr (exit 1) with the same code,
message and details as the `q` refusal, which prints on stdout; the CLI
re-encodes the envelope (indented, struct key order), so bytes differ.
**Compatibility:** consumers that read stories or epics from
`phases[].elements` or `criticalPath` must switch to `phases[].containers`;
treat absent `containers` and `task_cycles` as an older server and fall back
to task-only waves; match the in-scope refusal on
`error.details.reason == "task_dependency_cycle"`, not on the message text.

### Plan dependency diagnostics

The JSON `plan()` result includes `plan.projection_cycles` (an empty array when
none exist). Each entry contains container `members`, a closed `chain`, inducing
stored `links`, and projected `edges` with their `sources`. Projection cycles
are purely advisory and never block the task-graph order. Compact output emits
one advisory line when projection cycles exist. **Compatibility:** the Go plan
model's `CycleNodes` now carries task/bug cycles only; consumers must use
`projection_cycles` for container cycles.

Cross-parent task/bug links derive story/epic edges at read time as
`derivedBlockedBy` / `derivedBlocks`; they do not write container links. Explicit
legacy container links remain stored as `blockedBy` / `blocks` and participate
in container ordering. Active derived waiting sets `isAdvisoryBlocked`; active
explicit blocking sets `isBlocked`. Task link/unlink and reparenting preserve
explicit container links.

`task-board validate` reports `PROJECTION_CYCLE_DETECTED` as an advisory warning
for container cycles (including those involving explicit legacy links),
`TASK_CYCLE_DETECTED` as an error for task/bug cycles, and
`UNSUPPORTED_CONTAINER_LINK` as a separate warning for stored container links
without task-level support. A task DAG can have a container projection cycle
and still produce a plan. `task-board repair-links` lists unsupported stored
container links; confirmation removes only selected links still unsupported at
write time, never derived edges or task-cycle links. See the
[storage and repair contract](file-formats.md#container-dependency-projection).

**IMPORTANT:** Canonical planning lives in `task-board q 'plan(...)'`. Use `task-board plan` only as a local pretty-print / render / save facade over that same planning model. Never manually create phase breakdowns or write `.planning/` files from scratch.

